## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- MinHash sketches for Jaccard similarity
- Batch processing costs vs per-element costs
- Trade-offs: d (accuracy) vs memory, impact of collisions
- Exact vs approximate counting implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: initialize sketches for A and B
class MinHash {
  constructor(d, maxHash) { this.d = d; this.sig = Array(d).fill(Infinity); this.maxHash = maxHash; }
  add(x) { for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) { const h = hash_i(x, i) % this.maxHash; if (h < this.sig[i]) this.sig[i] = h; } }
  resembles(other) { let matches = 0; for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) if (this.sig[i] === other.sig[i]) matches++; return matches / this.d; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose d given target accuracy ε?
- How handle skewed data or duplicates in batches?
- How would you extend to weighted or multi-set similarity?

